WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.275
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.870
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome to a
special episode of Chalk Radio,

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.550
a podcast about
inspired teaching at MIT

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.710
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.195
I'm your host Sarah Hansen.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.460
In this episode, we want
to share some insights

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.230
into the many ways
we can transition

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.980
our traditional learning
environments into remote ones.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.080
We connected with
someone whose work even

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.960
before the pandemic hit
has been deeply focused on

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.300
how learning is changing.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.310
Our conversation
started off with what's

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.110
become a familiar sound for
listeners with young ones

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:35.610
at home.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.527
SPEAKER 2: [children
playing, family talking].

00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:40.510
SPEAKER 3: Well,
it looks like, ...

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.303
SARAH HANSEN: Just
so you know, you

00:00:42.303 --> 00:00:44.220
might hear some more of
this in the background

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.780
throughout the episode.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.780
We're all doing the best we
can working from home, right?

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.863
JUSTIN REICH: Hey, girls--

00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:51.360
SPEAKER 2: Dance party.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.650
SARAH HANSEN: Anyhow,
let's jump right in.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.160
JUSTIN REICH: I'm Justin Reich.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.120
So I'm an assistant professor
in Comparative Media Studies.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.120
And I run a lab called
the Teaching Systems Lab.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.070
And the mission of the
Teaching Systems Lab

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.140
is to design,
implement, and research

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.943
the future of teacher learning.

00:01:06.943 --> 00:01:08.860
And we try to figure out
new ways for teachers

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.485
to learn in online
and blended ways.

00:01:11.485 --> 00:01:13.750
SARAH HANSEN: In his
work, Justin partners

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.010
with teachers and instructors,
curriculum designers, software

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.030
developers, researchers, and
experts from across disciplines

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.080
to help teachers adapt to the
changing world of learning.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.750
Since the pandemic has
shifted almost all teaching

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.210
to remote instruction,
Justin's work

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.120
has adapted to the new
needs of instructors.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.490
JUSTIN REICH: I would say the
bulk of my teaching energy

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.820
has gone into helping K-12
educators and education

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.080
policymakers across the country.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.810
But most of my work
has been trying

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.470
to provide some guidance for
schools and school leaders

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.702
figuring out what distance
learning can look like.

00:01:51.702 --> 00:01:54.220
SARAH HANSEN: So for schools
and teachers with little

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.460
or even no experience
with online instruction,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.640
where do they even begin to
approach remote learning?

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.830
With so much changing
each day, what

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.720
are the most important
things educators

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.310
should be thinking about?

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.009
JUSTIN REICH: These are
enormously complicated issues

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.540
to deal with while you're
trying to feed millions more

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.550
meals to kids than
you ever had before

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.760
in large districts or
big urban school systems

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.370
while you're trying to
help your entire faculty

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.350
transition almost
instantaneously

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:24.860
into a new model of learning.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.943
I mean, doing all of these
things at the same time

00:02:26.943 --> 00:02:28.840
is just immensely complicated.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.960
So I would encourage
people to keep it simple.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.520
That's one principle.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.290
A second principle
is to really think

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.030
about how you can partner
with students and in K12

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:41.450
with their families.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.580
So the coronavirus
feels like it's

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.427
something being done to us.

00:02:45.427 --> 00:02:48.010
It feels like something that we
have very little control over.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.660
But our response
to the coronavirus

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.580
can be something that
we build together.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.140
So I would encourage education
leaders at every level from

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.580
college provosts to elementary
school teachers to school

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.770
principals, superintendents
-- whatever it is--

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.980
asking and partnering with
everybody in the system

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.670
is a really important part of
responding to remote learning.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.950
What most emergency remote
learning should look like

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.380
is kind of a package of
asynchronous materials

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.035
that's coupled with lots
of frequent check-ins.

00:03:17.035 --> 00:03:20.350
SARAH HANSEN: Synchronous
versus asynchronous teaching

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.650
or offering learning
experiences at a specific time

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.690
versus providing students
with the material

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.080
to engage with on
their own time is

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.436
at the core of Justin's
message for teachers.

00:03:31.436 --> 00:03:33.880
JUSTIN REICH: So in
lots of circumstances,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.520
synchronous learning is not
going to work for people.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.250
It's hard for all of us
to line up our schedules.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.270
In K12 circumstances, you've
got a teacher who's got a first

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.970
grader, a fourth grader, and
a seventh grader who's trying

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.033
to teach her kids who
are ninth graders,

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:50.450
but they have siblings or third
graders and fourth graders

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:51.257
and 12th graders...

00:03:51.257 --> 00:03:53.590
and trying to line up all
those schedules so that people

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.200
can be synchronously in
front of the one computer

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.350
that exists in a household or
to use the amount of bandwidth

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.680
because your home only allows
one synchronous video call

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.270
to happen at a time, it's
just too complicated.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.150
So for the most
part, what educators

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.660
should be thinking of setting
up is some kind of package

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.240
of curriculum that lasts
about a week or two

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.640
maybe with some sort of daily
guides of what people might

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.430
be doing each day recognizing
that people might have

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:19.899
to do two days of
work in one day

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.300
because they get busy with
other stuff for another day,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.950
or things like that
and then providing

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.360
frequent points of check-in.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.820
So a huge part of what
motivates us as learners

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.380
is when we're getting
feedback on what we're doing.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.810
And this doesn't have to be
based around grades or numbers

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:32.620
or other things like that.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.788
Just "that was a great idea.

00:04:33.788 --> 00:04:34.330
Keep that up.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.385
Tell me more about that."

00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:37.510
"Wow, it looks like you
did more on this assignment

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.560
than anyone you've done before.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.020
What else could
you do with this?

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.190
How could you take this further?

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.915
It seems like you're
interested in this."

00:04:43.915 --> 00:04:45.700
SARAH HANSEN:
Shifting so heavily

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.870
toward asynchronous teaching
might feel strange at first.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.410
But this one change may
address in a small way

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.680
some of the equity issues school
systems and individuals are

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.590
facing right now such
as differing access

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:00.940
to dependable Wi-Fi.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.590
JUSTIN REICH: There's
no survey that

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.980
was done in advance of the
pandemic that asked questions,

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:12.100
like, "do you have one device
for each school-aged child

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.793
or learner in your home
and a broadband connection

00:05:15.793 --> 00:05:17.710
and a printer to be able
to print things out?"

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.650
Like, we actually don't
know how well families

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.360
are equipped for this pandemic.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.310
But it's pretty clear that a
lot of our low-income housing

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:27.543
is in broadband deserts
or isn't networked in.

00:05:27.543 --> 00:05:29.710
I mean, there's lots of
people in urban environments

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.050
who in their typical
daily lives can

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:36.700
get by with having one
mobile broadband device

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.350
in their house,
and that's plenty.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.990
But now if you've got three
school-aged kids at home,

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.690
your demands have just
increased substantially.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.380
But I think partnering
with students,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.290
I think, really
thinking about, who

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.380
are the most vulnerable
learners in our community?

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.030
There are some people who come
from affluent well-equipped

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.020
homes.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.370
And they're able to weather this
crisis in a very different way

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.203
than if you're going back into
a poverty-impacted place that

00:06:02.203 --> 00:06:04.120
is really feeling the
effects of the recession

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.012
and the coronavirus.

00:06:05.012 --> 00:06:06.970
I think the students that
aren't reaching out--

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.730
I think the ones that
are not doing well,

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:12.635
I think we always had
this responsibility.

00:06:12.635 --> 00:06:14.010
But I think during
a pandemic, we

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:16.750
have a special responsibility
to reach out to those students

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.920
and say, "how can I
help you with this?

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.163
How can I keep you on track?"

00:06:20.163 --> 00:06:21.580
SARAH HANSEN: And
helping students

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.320
stay on track is another
big part of the puzzle

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.190
for a lot of educators
out there, especially

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.965
for classes that form the
foundations for later learning.

00:06:29.965 --> 00:06:31.840
JUSTIN REICH: I mean,
I think the other thing

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.715
that universities and
other places need to do

00:06:33.715 --> 00:06:37.240
is be strategic about
what it is that we want

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.830
students to be able to learn.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.470
So if you were to go
across the OCW catalog,

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.740
I think you would find at
least two types of courses.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:48.100
One type of course is where
to study a discipline you

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.200
sample from a canon.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.470
So literature is the most
obvious example of this.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.990
But I think it appears in
STEM classes and other places

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:56.740
as well.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.650
If you were to take a
Shakespeare class at MIT,

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.590
you wouldn't read all
of Shakespeare's work.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.540
You would read some selected
sample of that work.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.400
And we would probably
be willing to say

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.310
we read Romeo and
Juliet and Twelfth Night

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.530
and a couple of
sonnets before we left.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.540
And we were going to
do that much work again

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:14.650
in the fourth quarter.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:17.262
But actually, this time, we're
just going to read Henry the V.

00:07:17.262 --> 00:07:18.970
And we'll do it a
little bit more slowly.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.380
And that'll be fine.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.120
That is a less of a
sampling of the canon

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.520
that we would have
done previously.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.770
But it's a perfectly reasonable
sampling of the canon.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.520
We'll give you your
emergency pass grade.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.630
Everything is fine.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.640
There are other courses
in which there's

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.360
a sequential building of
material of understanding

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.420
in which subsequent courses
depend upon that sampling

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:38.230
understanding.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.690
So our Introduction
to Statistics Class,

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:43.120
it's not suitable to
say to a student, ah,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.873
we're just going to do half
of multiple regression.

00:07:45.873 --> 00:07:48.040
And we're just not going
to teach you the other half

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.290
or figure it out on their own.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.700
I mean, one of the things
that I think at the department

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.350
level or other levels
people need to do

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.990
is to say there's some courses
that we strategically reduced.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.990
And those young people
will be fine for the rest

00:07:58.990 --> 00:07:59.580
of their lives.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.770
We don't have to worry about it.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.228
There are other courses that
we had to strategically reduce

00:08:03.228 --> 00:08:03.820
in a pandemic.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.730
But we've got to figure
out how we support students

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.580
in learning that material
so they don't show up

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.010
at Intermediate
Statistics in the fall

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.900
without the preparation
that they need

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.150
to be successful and still,
the sort of lingering

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:17.180
effects of the pandemic.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.910
So that's a kind of
strategic question.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.240
It's probably best resolved
at the department level--

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.120
something along the lines
of, who are the students

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.780
in our core classes that we
think missed learning materials

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.730
that they really
are going to need?

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.884
And how are we going to
supplement over the summer

00:08:32.884 --> 00:08:35.590
or right when people come
back in August or in the fall

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:39.039
or whatever the new model
looks like to identify

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:43.485
the areas in which it's not OK
to just strategically reduce.

00:08:43.485 --> 00:08:44.860
But instead, we
got to figure out

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.735
how we help people get
the learning that they

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:49.876
need to be successful
in subsequent courses.

00:08:49.876 --> 00:08:51.370
SARAH HANSEN: When
I asked Justin

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:54.370
about putting new teaching
approaches into practice

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.650
and developing materials
to support educators

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:00.010
during COVID-19, we got
to talking about education

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:01.360
at scale.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.930
From the system as a whole to
each individual participant,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.280
there's such a wide range of
needs and no one prescription

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.103
for addressing them all.

00:09:11.103 --> 00:09:12.520
And in a time when
teachers should

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.630
be focusing on partnering
with students and families

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.560
and identifying how
to strategically

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:20.800
reduce and recover
content area material,

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.895
open educational resources may
have an important role to play.

00:09:24.895 --> 00:09:27.940
JUSTIN REICH: Students differ
so much from place to place.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:29.500
One of the things
that I'm seeing

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.060
is that a lot of
big districts are

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:35.230
creating sample lessons,
sample schedules--

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.610
those kinds of things.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.100
But my initial evidence
is that a lot of teachers

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.380
aren't using them, not
necessarily because they're

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.410
bad or wrong, but
just because they,

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.140
like, aren't right for where
their kids are right now.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.480
And so a lot of
teachers feel the need

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.500
to be basically inventing
their own new distance learning

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:57.040
curriculum as they're learning
a whole series of new practices

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:59.080
and co-inventing them
with their students

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.230
and finding the forty
percent of their kids

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.470
who aren't answering emails
or responding to phone calls

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:08.800
and facilitating their own
kids' instruction at home.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.140
I mean, I think that's
something that people really

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:12.470
need to keep in mind.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:15.550
But I would say that if
schools are facing waves

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:19.510
of closure in the fall or other
kinds of challenges like this,

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.610
I would really
encourage people to say,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:24.880
even though it's not exactly the
way I would want to teach it,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.130
there may be a better
comparative advantage

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:29.500
for faculty members to
focus on that check-in,

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.140
that facilitation role
rather than trying

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:37.118
to generate original content
by video without a studio

00:10:37.118 --> 00:10:39.160
and without all the other
resources that we need,

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:43.330
or consider doing less of
that, consider saying, "oh,

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.100
in my solid state
chemistry course,

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.370
I can find these MIT
OpenCourseWare videos.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:51.780
And six of the 10
lectures are great.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.983
And I'm going to only
record four for the period

00:10:53.983 --> 00:10:56.400
that we have to miss in the
fall" or other kinds of things

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.130
like that.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:00.900
I mean, I think one of the
things that we've discovered

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:05.910
is that faculty find it
very difficult to identify

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:09.780
whole online courses that they
like to just take and sort

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.500
of use as textbooks.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.280
But it strikes me that
a pandemic is exactly

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:17.670
the sort of time in which
people might want to employ

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.080
that kind of thinking.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.120
SARAH HANSEN: One of the
most important factors

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.610
in determining our
best way forward

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.560
is looking at how
we actually measure

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.780
the success of remote learning.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.060
Who do we look to for
guidance and models

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:32.010
of what is best right now?

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.370
It's not as simple
as it might seem.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.450
JUSTIN REICH: So we
just did this report

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.180
about the remote
learning guidance offered

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.427
by all 50 states.

00:11:40.427 --> 00:11:42.510
And one of the cautions
that we have in the report

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:46.260
is there are good reasons to
believe that lots of teachers

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.740
are not doing what this says,
that educational systems are

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.300
what we call loosely coupled.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:52.830
So there are
tightly-coupled systems

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.480
in our society
like the military.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.330
If a general in the military
says everyone's hair should

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.690
be shorter than x inches,
then within weeks,

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.670
there would be
hundreds of thousands

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.980
of soldiers around the
world whose hair is shorter

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.990
than those number of inches.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.490
Teaching is not like that.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:12.030
Education policymakers, leaders
all the time make edicts.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.430
And teachers sift through them.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.820
And they apply them as they
see fit in their own context

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.410
for a variety of reasons.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:22.020
And because of the incredible
granularity of teaching,

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.000
in a lot of cases,
we just don't know

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.590
what's happening at
the classroom level.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.380
I think most of
the places that we

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:32.400
say are doing a good
job of remote learning,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:36.240
their primary characteristic
is that they are affluent.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:38.790
One way you can do a good
job of remote learning

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.340
is have a bunch of
kids in stable homes

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.320
with broadband
internet connections

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.330
who each have their own device.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.940
And if those conditions
exist, then you

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.460
will have a thing that looks
a lot like good learning.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.640
If those conditions don't exist,
regardless of the heroic effort

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.530
of teachers, you'll
have things that look

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:57.210
like less effective learning.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:13:00.000
The way that my colleague
Tressie McMillan Cottom

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.920
said this is like
the number one thing

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:06.300
that education has to reduce
inequality is the school.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.430
The school is the
thing that we've lost.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.250
And there's not a
mechanism to like, rapidly

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.520
make up those sort
of equalizing forces.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:18.120
So that's a long way of
saying that I don't think

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.830
I know of places that
I would say, wow,

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:24.360
these folks are doing a really
great job addressing this.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:28.560
I think one measure that people
are using to define a good job

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.080
is who can do
things most quickly?

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:34.170
Who can get the thing
that looks closest

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.480
to what school looked
like before up and running

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:37.680
the fastest?

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.750
I'm not convinced
at the end of this

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:44.110
that that will end up being
a good proxy for better.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:45.900
SARAH HANSEN: And
in the end, it seems

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.840
like better learning while
we are at home simply

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.480
looks different than
what we're used to.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.290
And the more we
can embrace that,

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:59.432
the better off we as parents,
students, and educators may be.

00:13:59.432 --> 00:14:03.170
JUSTIN REICH: I've encouraged
both families and educators

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:05.900
to, if possible, not put
too much stock in trying

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:07.760
to recreate school at home.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.920
There are lots of
things that schools

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.060
can do in their local
environments that

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:15.090
can be really hard to replicate
in a home environment.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.980
But there's lots of learning
that can happen at home.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.967
And what we need to
do as best we can,

00:14:20.967 --> 00:14:22.550
cultivate and celebrate
that learning.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:24.440
I was interviewed
recently by a reporter

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.530
from The Arctic Sounder, which
is the newspaper of the north

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.120
slope of Alaska--

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.090
very bad connectivity up there.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.010
And so remote
learning is not going

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.220
to look like what it looks like
in an affluent Boston suburb.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.720
And what a lot of teachers there
were saying is, like, look,

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.860
this is a great
time for families

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.980
to strengthen their connection
to traditional Inupiaq values.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:49.040
This is a great time for kids
to learn how to cook and bake

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:52.790
and sew and bead and
repair snow machines

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:55.790
and do all the kinds of
learning that can happen really

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:56.810
well at home.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.970
So I think to families
out there that

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.370
are sort of struggling
during this period,

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:05.075
it is totally normal to
struggle during a pandemic.

00:15:05.075 --> 00:15:08.210
If you feel like this is hard
and is anxiety-provoking,

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:10.580
that is normal and lots
of people feel that way.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:11.998
And I think our goals--

00:15:11.998 --> 00:15:13.790
we can't put too much
pressure on ourselves

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:15.650
about keeping up and
staying up and things

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:17.360
like that-- think
about what can you

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:21.080
do to maximize the learning
and the resilience that

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.790
happens right now?

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.620
And if part of what
we do to do that is

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.320
like teach our kids
new chores, if it's

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:28.858
time for those nine
and 10-year-olds

00:15:28.858 --> 00:15:30.650
to start doing their
first load of laundry,

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:32.030
or their six or
seven-year-olds to do

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:33.530
their first sink
of dishes, like,

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.390
that's great
learning too that we

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:36.770
can do really well at the home.

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:39.350
And we can probably do
some reading and some math

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:42.800
and some social studies and
science along the way too.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.620
And some of that relates to
the first piece of advice

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:47.790
that I offered before,
which is let's partner

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:48.540
with our learners.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.900
And if those learners happen
to be our own kids, so much

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:52.483
the better to be
partnering with them.

00:16:02.645 --> 00:16:04.770
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
